IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `vcat-dates`

-- Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 -- jurisdiction (Part 3), procedure (Part 4), costs, settlement
-- offers and fees (Part 4 Divisions 8, 8A), orders (Division 9) and appeals (Part 5), with the Schedule 1 variations for
-- the claims a person most often brings to VCAT -- the questions of the PARTY.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who brings a claim or an application for review to VCAT, or answers one: the applicant (a person
-- seeking review of a decision about their compensation, a consumer with a small claim, a person who complains of
-- discrimination, a building owner) or the individual respondent. "Can I get reasons for the decision, and by when?",
-- "Can I have a lawyer?", "Will VCAT strike out my claim for the fee?", "Do I need leave to withdraw?", "Will I have to pay
-- the other side's costs?", "Did my settlement offer work?", "By when must I appeal?". The other party, the
-- decision-maker and the other actors the text names are what they are; VCAT, the principal registrar, the President and
-- the Supreme Court get no seat: their satisfactions, opinions, beliefs and orders are asked as facts.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list). The duties and steps with
-- deadlines (ss 45(2), 46(1), 114(1), (6), 117(2), 148(2), (3)) are DEONTIC rules run inside; no question takes a DEONTIC
-- or an EVENT.
--
-- Fees are "the prescribed fee" (ss 68, 70, 88): the regulations that prescribe them are not deposited, so no fee amount
-- is encoded. The Magistrates' Court's jurisdictional limit (s 121) is asked: it is set by another Act.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Victorian
-- Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 (Vic), Act No. 53/1998, Version No. 145 (28 July 2026), not the authorised
-- version. (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No
-- part may be reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of
-- 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `VCAT: the party's questions`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `The parties, the steps, and the kinds of proceeding`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `I`
    `the decision-maker`
    `the Tribunal`

DECLARE `Act done` IS ONE OF
    `request a written statement of reasons`
    `give a written statement of reasons`
    `request written reasons for the order`
    `accept the offer by signed notice`
    `apply for leave to appeal`
    `institute the appeal`

-- The kinds of proceeding that Schedule 1 varies for this focus (claims), and the two general kinds.
-- Sch 1 Pt 20 (cls 92-94) applies to "a proceeding under the Transport Accident Act 1986"; cl 93 only to "a proceeding for
-- review of a decision under section 77" of that Act. Sch 1 Pt 2AB (cls 4C-4I): "a proceeding relating to a small claim"
-- (the meaning in Chapter 7 of the Australian Consumer Law and Fair Trading Act 2012, a borrowed term). Sch 1 Pt 7
-- (cls 13-23): "a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010". Sch 1 Pt 6 cl 12B: "a domestic building dispute that
-- is a domestic building work dispute". s 115C(1)(c)-(e): the other proceedings with the fee presumption.
DECLARE `Kind of proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a review of a decision under section 77 of the Transport Accident Act 1986`
    `another proceeding under the Transport Accident Act 1986`
    `a proceeding relating to a small claim under the Australian Consumer Law and Fair Trading Act 2012`
    `a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010`
    `a domestic building work dispute under the Domestic Building Contracts Act 1995`
    `a proceeding under the Owners Corporations Act 2006 (other than a review under its section 191), under section 114, 120 or 149A of the Planning and Environment Act 1987, or of a kind prescribed for section 115C`
    `a review of a decision under another enabling enactment`
    `another proceeding in VCAT's original jurisdiction`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is under the Transport Accident Act` `the kind` IF
        `the kind` EQUALS `a review of a decision under section 77 of the Transport Accident Act 1986`
    OR  `the kind` EQUALS `another proceeding under the Transport Accident Act 1986`

-- s 42(1): "Review jurisdiction is jurisdiction ... to review a decision made by a decision-maker". A s 77 Transport
-- Accident Act application is a review (Sch 1 cl 93 calls it "a proceeding for review of a decision").
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a proceeding for review of a decision` `the kind` IF
        `the kind` EQUALS `a review of a decision under section 77 of the Transport Accident Act 1986`
    OR  `the kind` EQUALS `a review of a decision under another enabling enactment`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a small claim` `the kind` IF
    `the kind` EQUALS `a proceeding relating to a small claim under the Australian Consumer Law and Fair Trading Act 2012`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is under the Equal Opportunity Act` `the kind` IF
    `the kind` EQUALS `a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010`

GIVEN `the answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `yes` `the answer` IF
    CONSIDER `the answer`
    WHEN JUST b THEN b
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

-- The helper that runs a rule over what happened (as in the other Victorian baselines). On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an
-- EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH. It takes a DEONTIC value, so it is never exported.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
      `who` IS A `Party`
      `the act` IS A `Act done`
      `the time it was done` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `done in time under` `the rule` `by` `who` `doing` `the act` `at` `the time it was done` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time it was done`), (EVENT `who` `the act` `the time it was done`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Which jurisdiction (ss 40-43, 48)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Jurisdiction` IS ONE OF
    `original jurisdiction: VCAT decides the matter itself under the enabling enactment (ss 41, 43, 44)`
    `review jurisdiction: VCAT reviews a decision made by a decision-maker, with all the decision-maker's functions (ss 42(1), 48, 51)`

-- s 41 "Original jurisdiction is the jurisdiction of the Tribunal other than its review jurisdiction." s 42(2): Part 7 of
-- the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019 is original jurisdiction "for the avoidance of doubt".
GIVEN `to review a decision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `under Part 7 of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Jurisdiction`
`ss 41, 42 -- the jurisdiction` `to review a decision` `under Part 7 of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019` MEANS
    IF `to review a decision` AND (NOT `under Part 7 of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019`)
    THEN `review jurisdiction: VCAT reviews a decision made by a decision-maker, with all the decision-maker's functions (ss 42(1), 48, 51)`
    ELSE `original jurisdiction: VCAT decides the matter itself under the enabling enactment (ss 41, 43, 44)`

@export Is my VCAT proceeding in VCAT's original jurisdiction or its review jurisdiction (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 40-42)? Review jurisdiction is VCAT reviewing a decision someone else made (for example the Transport Accident Commission or a licensing body) under the Act that gives VCAT that power; everything else is original jurisdiction.
GIVEN `to review a decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are asking VCAT to review a decision that a decision-maker (a body, official or authority) has made about you, under an Act that gives VCAT power to review it
      `under Part 7 of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is under Part 7 of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019 (which is original jurisdiction even though it can involve looking at an earlier decision)
GIVETH A `Jurisdiction`
`Which jurisdiction is my proceeding in` MEANS
    `ss 41, 42 -- the jurisdiction` `to review a decision` `under Part 7 of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Reasons for the decision I want reviewed (ss 45-47, 49) -- if a decision-maker has decided something about me`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 45(2) "A request under subsection (1) must be made in writing within 28 days after the day on which the decision was
-- made." Run as a rule: I DO request WITHIN the days allowed, LEST the time runs out.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 45(2) -- request reasons within 28 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `request a written statement of reasons`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the request for a statement of reasons was not made within 28 days after the day on which the decision was made (s 45(2))"

GIVEN `the day of the decision` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 45(2) -- the last day to request reasons` `the day of the decision` MEANS
    `the date` 28 `days after` `the day of the decision`

@export What is the last day on which I can ask the decision-maker for a written statement of reasons for a decision I am entitled to have VCAT review (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 45)? The request must be in writing, within 28 days after the day on which the decision was made.
GIVEN `day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the decision was made (ISO date, e.g. 2026-08-03)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day to ask for reasons` MEANS
    `s 45(2) -- the last day to request reasons` `day of the decision`

-- s 45(1) "A person who is entitled to apply to the Tribunal for review of a decision ... may request": a scope limit
-- without "only" (NOTHING when not entitled). s 45(2) "must be made in writing within 28 days": a period the text fixes
-- (JUST FALSE outside it, ruling (a) of 2026-11-05) and a "must" of form (JUST FALSE when not in writing: fork F3).
GIVEN `entitled to review` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE
      `the day of the request` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 45 -- my request for reasons is valid` `entitled to review` `in writing` `the day of the decision` `the day of the request` MEANS
    IF NOT `entitled to review`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF NOT `in writing`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `the day` `the day of the request` `is before` `the day of the decision`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 45(2) -- request reasons within 28 days` 28) `by` `I` `doing` `request a written statement of reasons` `at` (`the days from` `the day of the decision` `to` `the day of the request`))

@export Was my request to the decision-maker for a statement of reasons made properly and in time (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 45)? Only a person entitled to apply to VCAT for review of the decision may ask; the request must be in writing and made within 28 days after the day the decision was made. No answer is given if you are not entitled to apply for review.
GIVEN `entitled to apply for review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are entitled to apply to VCAT for review of the decision, or to have it referred to VCAT for review
      `request in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the request in writing
      `day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the decision was made (ISO date)
      `day of the request` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you made the request (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was my request for reasons in time` MEANS
    `s 45 -- my request for reasons is valid` `entitled to apply for review` `request in writing` `day of the decision` `day of the request`

-- s 46(1) "A decision-maker must give a written statement of reasons ... as soon as practical, and in any event within 28
-- days or such other period as is specified in the enabling enactment, after receiving a request under section 45."
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 46(1) -- the decision-maker must give reasons` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the decision-maker`
    MUST `give a written statement of reasons`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the decision-maker` BECAUSE "the written statement of reasons was not given within 28 days (or the period the enabling enactment specifies) after the request was received (s 46(1))"

GIVEN `the other period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 46(1) -- the days for reasons` `the other period` MEANS
    IF `the other period` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN `the other period`
    ELSE 28

@export By when must the decision-maker give me its written statement of reasons (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 46(1))? As soon as practical, and in any event within 28 days after it receives your request, unless the Act that gives VCAT review power sets another period.
GIVEN `day request received` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the decision-maker received your request for reasons (ISO date)
      `other period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The period for giving reasons that the Act under which the decision was made specifies instead of 28 days, in days; 0 if it specifies none
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the decision-maker give me reasons` MEANS
    `the date` (`s 46(1) -- the days for reasons` `other period in days`) `days after` `day request received`

DECLARE `My statement of reasons` IS ONE OF
    `not required: the decision-maker had already given me a written statement with its reasons and its findings on material questions of fact (s 46(3))`
    `given in time (s 46(1))`
    `given, but after the period in s 46(1) ended`
    `not yet due: the period in s 46(1) has not ended`
    `overdue: not given within the period in s 46(1); I may apply to VCAT for an order that the decision-maker give it, and must give the decision-maker notice of my application (s 47(1), (2))`

GIVEN `already given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day received` IS A DATE
      `the other period` IS A NUMBER
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day given` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `My statement of reasons`
`ss 46, 47 -- my statement of reasons` `already given` `the day received` `the other period` `given` `the day given` `today` MEANS
    IF `already given`
    THEN `not required: the decision-maker had already given me a written statement with its reasons and its findings on material questions of fact (s 46(3))`
    ELSE IF `given`
         THEN IF `done in time under` (`s 46(1) -- the decision-maker must give reasons` `the days`) `by` `the decision-maker` `doing` `give a written statement of reasons` `at` (`the days from` `the day received` `to` `the day given`)
              THEN `given in time (s 46(1))`
              ELSE `given, but after the period in s 46(1) ended`
         ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is on or before` (`the date` `the days` `days after` `the day received`)
              THEN `not yet due: the period in s 46(1) has not ended`
              ELSE `overdue: not given within the period in s 46(1); I may apply to VCAT for an order that the decision-maker give it, and must give the decision-maker notice of my application (s 47(1), (2))`
    WHERE
        `the days` MEANS `s 46(1) -- the days for reasons` `the other period`

@export Has the decision-maker given me its statement of reasons in time, and if not, what can I do (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 46, 47)? If the statement is not given within 28 days (or the enabling Act's period) after your request was received, you may apply to VCAT for an order that it be given.
GIVEN `already given a full written statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker had already given you (with the decision or separately) a written statement of its reasons and its findings on material questions of fact, with the evidence they rest on
      `day request received` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the decision-maker received your request for reasons (ISO date)
      `other period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The period for giving reasons that the Act under which the decision was made specifies instead of 28 days, in days; 0 if none
      `statement given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker has now given you the written statement of reasons
      `day statement given` IS A DATE @desc The day on which it was given (ISO date); any date if it has not been given
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO date)
GIVETH A `My statement of reasons`
`Have I been given reasons in time` MEANS
    `ss 46, 47 -- my statement of reasons` `already given a full written statement` `day request received` `other period in days` `statement given` `day statement given` `today`

-- s 49(2) "Copies must be lodged ... within 28 days after (a) the day on which the decision-maker received notice of the
-- application to the Tribunal; or (b) the day on which the decision-maker referred the decision to the Tribunal".
@export By when must the decision-maker lodge with VCAT its statement of reasons and every other document it considers relevant to my review (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 49)? Within 28 days after it received notice of your application (or after it referred the decision to VCAT).
GIVEN `day decision-maker received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the decision-maker received notice of your application to VCAT, or referred the decision to VCAT (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the decision-maker lodge the material` MEANS
    `the date` 28 `days after` `day decision-maker received notice`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `While VCAT reviews a decision, and if I miss the hearing (ss 50, 51(5), 99(2))`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `The decision while it is reviewed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: starting the review does not affect the decision; it keeps operating and can be put into effect unless VCAT stays it (s 50(1), (3))`
    `no: VCAT has made an order staying the decision (s 50(2)(b), (3))`
    `not covered: the enabling enactment provides otherwise; its own rule decides (s 50(2)(a))`

GIVEN `the enactment provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
      `stayed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The decision while it is reviewed`
`s 50 -- the decision while it is reviewed` `the enactment provides otherwise` `stayed` MEANS
    IF `the enactment provides otherwise`
    THEN `not covered: the enabling enactment provides otherwise; its own rule decides (s 50(2)(a))`
    ELSE IF `stayed`
         THEN `no: VCAT has made an order staying the decision (s 50(2)(b), (3))`
         ELSE `yes: starting the review does not affect the decision; it keeps operating and can be put into effect unless VCAT stays it (s 50(1), (3))`

@export Does the decision I have asked VCAT to review still operate while the review goes on (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 50)? Yes, unless the Act under which it was made says otherwise or VCAT orders a stay; you or any party may ask VCAT for a stay.
GIVEN `enabling Act provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act under which the decision was made provides that the decision is suspended (or otherwise affected) while it is reviewed
      `VCAT has stayed the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has made an order staying the operation of the decision
GIVETH A `The decision while it is reviewed`
`Does the decision operate during the review` MEANS
    `s 50 -- the decision while it is reviewed` `enabling Act provides otherwise` `VCAT has stayed the decision`

DECLARE `If I miss the hearing` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise: I appeared, personally or by a representative`
    `VCAT must confirm the decision under review (s 51(5))`
    `the hearing may be held in my absence, as I was given notice of it in accordance with the rules (s 99(2))`
    `not covered: s 99(2) speaks only of a person given notice in accordance with the rules`

-- s 51(5) "If an applicant does not appear (personally or by representative) at the hearing of a proceeding for review of
-- a decision, the Tribunal must confirm the decision." It has no notice condition (finding 2). s 99(2) "If a person ... to
-- whom notice has been given in accordance with the rules fails to attend, the hearing may be held in the absence of that
-- person": a scope limit, NOTHING outside it.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `I am the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `appeared` IS A BOOLEAN
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `If I miss the hearing`
`ss 51(5), 99(2) -- if I miss the hearing` `the kind` `I am the applicant` `appeared` `notice given` MEANS
    IF `appeared`
    THEN `does not arise: I appeared, personally or by a representative`
    ELSE IF `is a proceeding for review of a decision` `the kind` AND `I am the applicant`
         THEN `VCAT must confirm the decision under review (s 51(5))`
         ELSE IF `notice given`
              THEN `the hearing may be held in my absence, as I was given notice of it in accordance with the rules (s 99(2))`
              ELSE `not covered: s 99(2) speaks only of a person given notice in accordance with the rules`

@export What happens if I do not come to the VCAT hearing, personally or by a representative (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 51(5), 99(2))? If you applied for review of a decision, VCAT must confirm the decision. Otherwise, if you were given notice, the hearing may go ahead without you. (You may be able to ask VCAT to re-open an order made in your absence: s 120.)
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is: a Transport Accident Act s 77 review; another Transport Accident Act proceeding; a small claim under the Australian Consumer Law and Fair Trading Act 2012; an Equal Opportunity Act 2010 proceeding; a domestic building work dispute; an owners corporations or listed planning proceeding; a review of a decision under another Act; or another proceeding in VCAT's original jurisdiction
      `I am the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the applicant (the person who brought the proceeding)
      `I appeared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you appeared at the hearing, personally or by a representative
      `I was given notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given notice of the time and place of the hearing in accordance with VCAT's rules
GIVETH A `If I miss the hearing`
`What happens if I miss the hearing` MEANS
    `ss 51(5), 99(2) -- if I miss the hearing` `kind of proceeding` `I am the applicant` `I appeared` `I was given notice`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Representation and help at VCAT (ss 62, 63, 63A, Sch 1 cl 4D)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 62(1) "a party (a) may appear personally; or (b) may be represented by a professional advocate if (i) the party is a
-- person referred to in subsection (2) [for an individual: (a) a child]; or (ii) another party ... is a professional
-- advocate; or (iii) another party ... who is permitted under this section to be represented by a professional advocate
-- is so represented; or (iv) all the parties ... agree; or (c) may be represented by any person (including a professional
-- advocate) permitted or specified by the Tribunal." A closed "if" list: JUST FALSE when no head holds.
-- Sch 1 cl 4D: in a small claim "may be represented by a professional advocate only if (a) the Tribunal is satisfied
-- that no other party ... will be unfairly disadvantaged ...; and (b) either (i) all parties ... agree; or (ii) the
-- Tribunal directs that the representation be allowed"; s 62(1)(b) does not apply.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `a child` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another party is a professional advocate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another permitted party is represented by one` IS A BOOLEAN
      `all parties agree` IS A BOOLEAN
      `VCAT permits or directs it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `VCAT is satisfied no party is unfairly disadvantaged` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 62, Sch 1 cl 4D -- I may be represented by a lawyer` `the kind` `a child` `another party is a professional advocate` `another permitted party is represented by one` `all parties agree` `VCAT permits or directs it` `VCAT is satisfied no party is unfairly disadvantaged` MEANS
    IF `is a small claim` `the kind`
    THEN JUST (    `VCAT is satisfied no party is unfairly disadvantaged`
               AND (`all parties agree` OR `VCAT permits or directs it`))
    ELSE JUST (    `a child`
               OR  `another party is a professional advocate`
               OR  `another permitted party is represented by one`
               OR  `all parties agree`
               OR  `VCAT permits or directs it`)

@export Can I be represented at VCAT by a lawyer or other professional advocate (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 62, Sch 1 cl 4D)? Generally a party appears in person, but may have a professional advocate if the party is a child, another party is or has one as permitted, all parties agree, or VCAT permits. In a consumer small claim, only if VCAT is satisfied no other party will be unfairly disadvantaged and either all parties agree or VCAT directs it.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is (a consumer small claim has its own rule)
      `I am a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a child
      `another party is a professional advocate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another party to the proceeding is a professional advocate (a lawyer, former lawyer, law clerk, law graduate, or a person VCAT considers an experienced advocate)
      `another party is represented by one as permitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another party who is allowed a professional advocate under s 62 (for example a council, a government body, a credit provider, a domestic building insurer, or a child) is represented by one
      `all parties agree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties agree that you may have a professional advocate
      `VCAT permits it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has permitted (or, in a small claim, directed) that you be represented by the person
      `VCAT satisfied no unfair disadvantage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a consumer small claim only: whether VCAT is satisfied that no other party will be unfairly disadvantaged if you are represented
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be represented by a lawyer` MEANS
    `s 62, Sch 1 cl 4D -- I may be represented by a lawyer` `kind of proceeding` `I am a child` `another party is a professional advocate` `another party is represented by one as permitted` `all parties agree` `VCAT permits it` `VCAT satisfied no unfair disadvantage`

-- s 63 "Unless the Tribunal directs otherwise, a party may be assisted ... by an interpreter".
@export Can I have an interpreter (or another person needed to make the proceeding understandable to me) at VCAT (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 63)? Yes, unless VCAT directs otherwise.
GIVEN `VCAT directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has directed that you may not be assisted by an interpreter or that person
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have an interpreter` MEANS
    IF `VCAT directed otherwise`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 63A(1) "Unless the Tribunal directs otherwise, a party may be assisted ... by a support person"; (2) "A support person
-- ... must not (a) be a party to the proceeding; or (b) represent a party to the proceeding."
@export Can I bring a support person (for example a family member or friend) to support me at VCAT (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 63A)? Yes, unless VCAT directs otherwise; the support person must not be a party or represent a party.
GIVEN `VCAT directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has directed that you may not be assisted by a support person
      `the person is a party or a representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you want as your support person is a party to the proceeding, or represents a party
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have a support person` MEANS
    IF `VCAT directed otherwise` OR `the person is a party or a representative`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `The application fee (ss 68, 132)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `My application and the fee` IS ONE OF
    `VCAT acts on my application: the fee is paid, waived, postponed, or reduced and the reduced fee paid (s 68(3), (3A))`
    `VCAT takes no further action on my application until the fee is paid, waived, or reduced and the reduced fee paid (s 68(3))`

-- s 68(3) "If the fee is not paid at the time the application is lodged, the Tribunal is to take no further action ...
-- until (a) the fee is paid; or (b) the fee is waived ...; or (c) if the fee is reduced ..., the reduced fee is paid."
-- (3A) it does not apply to an application whose fee is postponed.
GIVEN `paid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `waived` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reduced and the reduced fee paid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `postponed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `My application and the fee`
`s 68(3) -- my application and the fee` `paid` `waived` `reduced and the reduced fee paid` `postponed` MEANS
    IF `paid` OR `waived` OR `reduced and the reduced fee paid` OR `postponed`
    THEN `VCAT acts on my application: the fee is paid, waived, postponed, or reduced and the reduced fee paid (s 68(3), (3A))`
    ELSE `VCAT takes no further action on my application until the fee is paid, waived, or reduced and the reduced fee paid (s 68(3))`

@export Will VCAT act on my application if I have not paid the application fee (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 68(3))? Not until the fee is paid, or waived, or reduced and the reduced fee paid; an application whose fee is postponed goes ahead.
GIVEN `fee paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the application fee
      `fee waived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee has been waived
      `fee reduced and paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee was reduced and you have paid the reduced fee
      `fee postponed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether payment of the fee has been postponed
GIVETH A `My application and the fee`
`Will VCAT act on my application without the fee` MEANS
    `s 68(3) -- my application and the fee` `fee paid` `fee waived` `fee reduced and paid` `fee postponed`

DECLARE `Strike-out for the fee` IS ONE OF
    `no: the fee was paid within 30 days after the day I lodged the application (s 68(4))`
    `no: the fee was waived within the 30 days (s 68(4)(a))`
    `no: the fee was reduced and I paid the reduced fee within the 30 days (s 68(4)(b))`
    `no: I asked for the fee to be reduced, waived or postponed and my request had not been decided by the end of the 30 days (s 68(4)(c))`
    `not yet: the 30 days after the day I lodged the application have not ended (s 68(4))`
    `yes: VCAT may strike out my proceeding, as the fee was not paid within 30 days after the day I lodged the application (s 68(4))`
    `not yet: the date set for paying my postponed fee has not passed (s 68(5))`
    `no: I paid my postponed fee by the date set for payment (s 68(5))`
    `yes: VCAT may strike out my proceeding, as my postponed fee was not paid by the date set for payment (s 68(5))`

-- s 68(4) "If the fee is not paid within 30 days after the day on which the application is lodged, the Tribunal may make
-- an order striking out the proceeding, unless (a) the fee has been waived ... in that period; or (b) the fee has been
-- reduced ... and the reduced fee has been paid in that period; or (c) an applicant has requested the reduction, waiver or
-- postponement of the fee and the request has not been determined by the end of that period." (5) a postponed fee "not
-- paid by the date specified for payment". The last day of the 30 days is the lodging day plus 30 (fork F1).
GIVEN `the day lodged` IS A DATE
      `postponed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day due` IS A DATE
      `paid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day paid` IS A DATE
      `waived in the period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reduced and paid in the period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `request undecided at the end` IS A BOOLEAN
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Strike-out for the fee`
`s 68(4), (5) -- strike-out for the fee` `the day lodged` `postponed` `the day due` `paid` `the day paid` `waived in the period` `reduced and paid in the period` `request undecided at the end` `today` MEANS
    IF `postponed`
    THEN IF `paid` AND `the day` `the day paid` `is on or before` `the day due`
         THEN `no: I paid my postponed fee by the date set for payment (s 68(5))`
         ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is on or before` `the day due`
              THEN `not yet: the date set for paying my postponed fee has not passed (s 68(5))`
              ELSE `yes: VCAT may strike out my proceeding, as my postponed fee was not paid by the date set for payment (s 68(5))`
    ELSE IF `paid` AND `the day` `the day paid` `is on or before` `the last day`
         THEN `no: the fee was paid within 30 days after the day I lodged the application (s 68(4))`
         ELSE IF `waived in the period`
              THEN `no: the fee was waived within the 30 days (s 68(4)(a))`
              ELSE IF `reduced and paid in the period`
                   THEN `no: the fee was reduced and I paid the reduced fee within the 30 days (s 68(4)(b))`
                   ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
                        THEN `not yet: the 30 days after the day I lodged the application have not ended (s 68(4))`
                        ELSE IF `request undecided at the end`
                             THEN `no: I asked for the fee to be reduced, waived or postponed and my request had not been decided by the end of the 30 days (s 68(4)(c))`
                             ELSE `yes: VCAT may strike out my proceeding, as the fee was not paid within 30 days after the day I lodged the application (s 68(4))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the date` 30 `days after` `the day lodged`

@export Can VCAT strike out my proceeding because the application fee has not been paid (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 68(4), (5))? It may if the fee is not paid within 30 days after the day you lodged the application, unless within that time it was waived, or reduced and the reduced fee paid, or your request for a reduction, waiver or postponement was still undecided at the end of the 30 days. A postponed fee must be paid by the date set when it was postponed.
GIVEN `day lodged` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you lodged your application (ISO date)
      `fee postponed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether payment of the fee was postponed
      `day postponed fee due` IS A DATE @desc If the fee was postponed, the date set for paying it (ISO date); any date otherwise
      `fee paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the fee (the full fee, or a postponed fee)
      `day paid` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you paid it (ISO date); any date if not paid
      `waived within the 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee was waived within 30 days after the day you lodged
      `reduced and paid within the 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee was reduced and you paid the reduced fee within 30 days after the day you lodged
      `request undecided at the end of the 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for the fee to be reduced, waived or postponed and that request had still not been decided at the end of the 30 days
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO date)
GIVETH A `Strike-out for the fee`
`Can my proceeding be struck out for the fee` MEANS
    `s 68(4), (5) -- strike-out for the fee` `day lodged` `fee postponed` `day postponed fee due` `fee paid` `day paid` `waived within the 30 days` `reduced and paid within the 30 days` `request undecided at the end of the 30 days` `today`

-- s 132(1) "The principal registrar in any case may reduce, waive, postpone, remit or refund any fee ... if the principal
-- registrar considers that the payment of the fee would cause the person responsible for its payment financial hardship
-- or on any other prescribed ground in the regulations." An "if": JUST FALSE when neither holds. The principal
-- registrar's view is asked; the prescribed grounds (regulations, not deposited) are asked as one fact.
@export Can my VCAT fee be reduced, waived, postponed, remitted or refunded (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 132(1))? The principal registrar may do so if the registrar considers that paying it would cause you financial hardship, or on another ground the regulations prescribe.
GIVEN `registrar considers it a financial hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the principal registrar considers that paying the fee would cause you financial hardship
      `another prescribed ground applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another ground prescribed in the regulations for relief from the fee applies to you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my fee be waived` MEANS
    IF `registrar considers it a financial hardship` OR `another prescribed ground applies`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 132(2) "If costs are awarded by the Tribunal against a person who has had a fee reduced, waived ... the person becomes
-- liable to pay the amount of the fee"; (3) "not entitled, without leave of the Tribunal, to bring any proceeding, or make
-- any application in an existing proceeding, until the fee is paid".
@export If my VCAT fee was reduced, waived, postponed, remitted or refunded, must I now pay it after all (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 132(2), (3))? Yes, if VCAT awards costs against you; and until you pay it you cannot bring a proceeding, or make an application in an existing one, without VCAT's leave. No answer is given if your fee was never relieved.
GIVEN `my fee was relieved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a fee of yours was reduced, waived, postponed, remitted or refunded by the principal registrar
      `costs awarded against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has awarded costs against you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay back a relieved fee` MEANS
    IF NOT `my fee was relieved`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST `costs awarded against me`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Time to start, withdrawing, and dismissal (ss 74, 78, 126, Sch 1 cls 4E, 4F, 4I, 17, 18, 94)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 126(1) "The Tribunal ... may extend any time limit fixed by or under an enabling enactment for the commencement of a
-- proceeding"; (3) even after it expired; (4) "may not ... if to do so would cause any prejudice or detriment to a party or
-- potential party that cannot be remedied by an appropriate order for costs or damages". Sch 1 cl 4F: s 126(1) "does not
-- apply to a proceeding relating to a small claim"; cl 94: s 126(1) and (2)(a) "do not apply to a proceeding under the
-- Transport Accident Act 1986". Flat negatives: JUST FALSE.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `irremediable prejudice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 126 -- VCAT may extend my time to start` `the kind` `irremediable prejudice` MEANS
    IF `is under the Transport Accident Act` `the kind`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `is a small claim` `the kind`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `irremediable prejudice`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE JUST TRUE

@export Can VCAT extend the time limit for starting my proceeding, even if it has already passed (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 126, Sch 1 cls 4F, 94)? VCAT may extend a time limit that the enabling Act sets for starting a proceeding, unless extending it would cause another party prejudice that costs or damages cannot remedy. It cannot in a consumer small claim or a Transport Accident Act proceeding.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `prejudice that costs cannot remedy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether extending the time would cause a party or potential party prejudice or detriment that an order for costs or damages could not remedy
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can VCAT extend my time to start` MEANS
    `s 126 -- VCAT may extend my time to start` `kind of proceeding` `prejudice that costs cannot remedy`

DECLARE `Withdrawing my application` IS ONE OF
    `I may withdraw without VCAT's leave (Sch 1 cl 4E)`
    `I may withdraw without VCAT's leave (Sch 1 cl 17(1))`
    `I need VCAT's leave to withdraw before VCAT determines it (s 74(1))`
    `not covered: s 74(1) speaks of withdrawal before VCAT determines the application`

-- s 74(1) "If the Tribunal gives leave, an applicant may withdraw an application ... before it is determined". Sch 1
-- cl 4E: in a small claim "may withdraw that application without the leave of the Tribunal"; cl 17(1): in an Equal
-- Opportunity Act application "is not required to obtain the leave of the Tribunal to withdraw".
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `determined` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Withdrawing my application`
`s 74(1), Sch 1 cls 4E, 17 -- withdrawing my application` `the kind` `determined` MEANS
    IF `determined`
    THEN `not covered: s 74(1) speaks of withdrawal before VCAT determines the application`
    ELSE IF `is a small claim` `the kind`
         THEN `I may withdraw without VCAT's leave (Sch 1 cl 4E)`
         ELSE IF `is under the Equal Opportunity Act` `the kind`
              THEN `I may withdraw without VCAT's leave (Sch 1 cl 17(1))`
              ELSE `I need VCAT's leave to withdraw before VCAT determines it (s 74(1))`

@export Do I need VCAT's leave to withdraw my application (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 74(1), Sch 1 cls 4E, 17)? Generally yes, before VCAT determines it; not in a consumer small claim or an Equal Opportunity Act application. If you withdraw you must tell the other parties in writing (unless the principal registrar does).
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `already determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has already determined your application
GIVETH A `Withdrawing my application`
`Do I need leave to withdraw` MEANS
    `s 74(1), Sch 1 cls 4E, 17 -- withdrawing my application` `kind of proceeding` `already determined`

-- s 74(2)(b) "the Tribunal may make an order that the applicant pay all, or any part of, the costs of the other parties".
-- Sch 1 cl 4I(1) "The Tribunal cannot order costs in a proceeding relating to a small claim, except in a review of a
-- determination under section 120". s 51A(5) "cannot make an order for costs under section 74(2)(b) only because an
-- applicant withdraws a proceeding following reconsideration by the decision-maker". Sch 1 cl 17(2) keeps the power in
-- an Equal Opportunity Act proceeding. Sch 1 cl 92 disapplies s 109 only, not s 74(2)(b) (fork F4).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `a review of a s 120 determination` IS A BOOLEAN
      `only because of reconsideration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 74(2)(b) -- costs on withdrawal` `the kind` `a review of a s 120 determination` `only because of reconsideration` MEANS
    IF `is a small claim` `the kind` AND (NOT `a review of a s 120 determination`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `only because of reconsideration`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

@export If I withdraw my VCAT application, can I be ordered to pay the other parties' costs (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 51A(5), 74(2)(b), Sch 1 cls 4I, 17(2))? VCAT may order you to pay all or part of their costs, except in a consumer small claim, and not merely because you withdrew a review after the decision-maker reconsidered its decision at VCAT's invitation.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `a review of a section 120 determination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a small claim: whether the withdrawn application was for review (re-opening) of an order under s 120
      `withdrawn only because the decision-maker reconsidered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the only reason costs are sought is that you withdrew a review after the decision-maker reconsidered its decision at VCAT's invitation (s 51A)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to pay costs if I withdraw` MEANS
    `s 74(2)(b) -- costs on withdrawal` `kind of proceeding` `a review of a section 120 determination` `withdrawn only because the decision-maker reconsidered`

-- s 74(2)(d) "the applicant cannot make a further application ... in relation to the same facts and circumstances without
-- the leave of the Tribunal". Different facts: the section is silent (NOTHING).
@export After withdrawing a VCAT application, can I bring a new application on the same facts (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 74(2)(d))? Only with VCAT's leave. No answer is given for an application on different facts, which s 74 does not deal with.
GIVEN `same facts and circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the new application is about the same facts and circumstances as the one you withdrew
      `VCAT gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has given you leave to bring the new application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply again after withdrawing` MEANS
    IF NOT `same facts and circumstances`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST `VCAT gave leave`

-- Sch 1 cl 18 "The Tribunal may make an order under section 76 summarily dismissing an application under the Equal
-- Opportunity Act 2010 in respect of an alleged contravention of Part 4, 6, 6A or 7 of that Act if the alleged
-- contravention occurred more than 12 months before the application was made." The Parts are a scope limit (NOTHING
-- outside); the 12 months an "if" (JUST FALSE within them). "More than 12 months before" the application day: the
-- contravention day is before the same day 12 months before it, i.e. the application day is after the same day 12 months
-- after the contravention (fork F2).
GIVEN `in the listed Parts` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE
      `the day applied` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 cl 18 -- may be dismissed as more than 12 months old` `in the listed Parts` `the day of the contravention` `the day applied` MEANS
    IF NOT `in the listed Parts`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the day` (`the same day` 12 `months after` `the day of the contravention`) `is before` `the day applied`)

@export Can VCAT summarily dismiss my discrimination application because what I complain of happened more than 12 months before I applied (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 Sch 1 cl 18)? For a contravention of Part 4, 6, 6A or 7 of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (discrimination, sexual harassment, victimisation and related conduct), VCAT may dismiss the application if the contravention occurred more than 12 months before the application.
GIVEN `contravention of Part 4, 6, 6A or 7` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contravention you allege is of Part 4, 6, 6A or 7 of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010
      `day of the contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the alleged contravention occurred (ISO date)
      `day of the application` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you made your application to VCAT (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my discrimination application be dismissed as too old` MEANS
    `Sch 1 cl 18 -- may be dismissed as more than 12 months old` `contravention of Part 4, 6, 6A or 7` `day of the contravention` `day of the application`

DECLARE `Disadvantaging conduct` IS ONE OF
    `s 78 does not apply: VCAT does not believe a party is conducting the proceeding in a way that unnecessarily disadvantages another`
    `VCAT may dismiss or strike out the proceeding, and may order costs under s 109 (s 78(2)(a), (c))`
    `VCAT may decide the proceeding in the applicant's favour, or strike the party out, and may order costs under s 109 (s 78(2)(b), (c))`

-- s 78(1) "This section applies if the Tribunal believes that a party ... is conducting the proceeding in a way that
-- unnecessarily disadvantages another party ... by conduct such as" (a)-(g); (2) the orders.
GIVEN `VCAT believes it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the applicant causes it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Disadvantaging conduct`
`s 78 -- disadvantaging conduct` `VCAT believes it` `the applicant causes it` MEANS
    IF NOT `VCAT believes it`
    THEN `s 78 does not apply: VCAT does not believe a party is conducting the proceeding in a way that unnecessarily disadvantages another`
    ELSE IF `the applicant causes it`
         THEN `VCAT may dismiss or strike out the proceeding, and may order costs under s 109 (s 78(2)(a), (c))`
         ELSE `VCAT may decide the proceeding in the applicant's favour, or strike the party out, and may order costs under s 109 (s 78(2)(b), (c))`

@export What can VCAT do if a party (the other side, or I) conducts the proceeding in a way that unnecessarily disadvantages another party, for example by not following VCAT's orders, causing adjournments, trying to deceive, or not attending mediation or the hearing (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 78)? If the applicant is responsible, VCAT may dismiss the proceeding; if another party is, VCAT may decide it for the applicant or strike that party out; either way it may order costs.
GIVEN `VCAT believes the conduct disadvantages a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT believes that a party is conducting the proceeding in a way that unnecessarily disadvantages another party
      `the applicant is responsible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party responsible for the conduct is the applicant (the person who brought the proceeding)
GIVETH A `Disadvantaging conduct`
`What can VCAT do about disadvantaging conduct` MEANS
    `s 78 -- disadvantaging conduct` `VCAT believes the conduct disadvantages a party` `the applicant is responsible`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Settlement offers (ss 112-115, Sch 1 cls 22, 93) -- if an offer to settle has been made`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 112(1)(a) "a party to a proceeding (other than a proceeding for review of a decision)"; Sch 1 cl 93: "Despite section
-- 112(1)(a), sections 112 to 115 apply to a proceeding for review of a decision under section 77 of the Transport
-- Accident Act 1986"; cl 22: "Sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010".
-- s 113(4) "If an offer provides for the payment of money, the offer must specify when that money is to be paid."
-- s 114(2) "The minimum period that can be specified is 14 days."
GIVEN `money` IS A BOOLEAN
      `says when paid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a period specified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 113, 114 -- the offer complies` `money` `says when paid` `a period specified` `the period` IF
        ((NOT `money`) OR `says when paid`)
    AND ((NOT `a period specified`) OR `the period` AT LEAST 14)

DECLARE `My settlement offer` IS ONE OF
    `not covered: sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Sch 1 cl 22)`
    `does not comply: it is not in writing (s 112(1)(a))`
    `does not comply: it provides for the payment of money without saying when the money is to be paid (s 113(4))`
    `does not comply: it specifies a period for acceptance shorter than the minimum of 14 days (s 114(2))`
    `complies with sections 113 and 114 (an offer that does not say otherwise is made without prejudice: s 113(2))`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `money` IS A BOOLEAN
      `says when paid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a period specified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `My settlement offer`
`ss 112-114 -- the settlement offer` `the kind` `in writing` `money` `says when paid` `a period specified` `the period` MEANS
    IF `is under the Equal Opportunity Act` `the kind`
    THEN `not covered: sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Sch 1 cl 22)`
    ELSE IF NOT `in writing`
         THEN `does not comply: it is not in writing (s 112(1)(a))`
         ELSE IF `money` AND (NOT `says when paid`)
              THEN `does not comply: it provides for the payment of money without saying when the money is to be paid (s 113(4))`
              ELSE IF `a period specified` AND `the period` LESS THAN 14
                   THEN `does not comply: it specifies a period for acceptance shorter than the minimum of 14 days (s 114(2))`
                   ELSE `complies with sections 113 and 114 (an offer that does not say otherwise is made without prejudice: s 113(2))`

@export Does a written offer to settle my VCAT proceeding meet the Act's requirements, so that rejecting it can carry costs consequences (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 112-114, Sch 1 cl 22)? The offer must be in writing; an offer to pay money must say when it will be paid; and any period it gives for acceptance must be at least 14 days. These rules do not apply in an Equal Opportunity Act proceeding.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `offer in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer was made in writing
      `offer is to pay money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer provides for the payment of money
      `says when the money is paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer says when that money is to be paid
      `offer gives a period for acceptance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer specifies a period after which it can no longer be accepted
      `period for acceptance in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the offer gives for acceptance, counted from when it was made; 0 if it gives none
GIVETH A `My settlement offer`
`Does the settlement offer comply` MEANS
    `ss 112-114 -- the settlement offer` `kind of proceeding` `offer in writing` `offer is to pay money` `says when the money is paid` `offer gives a period for acceptance` `period for acceptance in days`

-- s 114(1) "An offer must be open for acceptance until immediately before the Tribunal makes its orders on the matters in
-- dispute, or until the expiry of a specified period after the offer is made, whichever is the shorter period." The last
-- day of a specified period is the day made plus the period (fork F1). (6) "A party can only accept an offer by giving the
-- party who made it a signed notice of acceptance."
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 114(1), (6) -- accept while the offer is open` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `accept the offer by signed notice`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the signed notice of acceptance was not given while the offer was open (s 114(1), (6))"

@export What is the last day on which a settlement offer in my VCAT proceeding can be accepted (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 114(1), (2))? The offer stays open for the period it specifies (at least 14 days after it was made) or until just before VCAT makes its orders, whichever ends first. No date is given for an offer that names no period (it stays open until just before VCAT's orders) or names one shorter than 14 days (it does not comply).
GIVEN `day offer made` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the offer was made (ISO date)
      `offer gives a period for acceptance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer specifies a period for acceptance
      `period for acceptance in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the offer gives for acceptance; 0 if none
      `VCAT has made its orders` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has made (or has fixed a day to make) its orders on the matters in dispute
      `day of VCAT's orders` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT made its orders (ISO date); any date if not made
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`What is the last day to accept the offer` MEANS
    IF NOT `offer gives a period for acceptance`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `period for acceptance in days` LESS THAN 14
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE IF `VCAT has made its orders` AND `the day` `day of VCAT's orders` `is on or before` `the end of the period`
              THEN JUST `day of VCAT's orders`
              ELSE JUST `the end of the period`
    WHERE
        `the end of the period` MEANS `the date` `period for acceptance in days` `days after` `day offer made`

GIVEN `the day made` IS A DATE
      `a period specified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period` IS A NUMBER
      `signed notice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of notice` IS A DATE
      `orders made before notice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 114 -- I accepted the offer in time` `the day made` `a period specified` `the period` `signed notice` `the day of notice` `orders made before notice` MEANS
    IF `a period specified` AND `the period` LESS THAN 14
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF NOT `signed notice`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `orders made before notice`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE IF `the day` `the day of notice` `is before` `the day made`
                   THEN JUST FALSE
                   ELSE IF NOT `a period specified`
                        THEN JUST TRUE
                        ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 114(1), (6) -- accept while the offer is open` `the period`) `by` `I` `doing` `accept the offer by signed notice` `at` (`the days from` `the day made` `to` `the day of notice`))

@export Did I accept a settlement offer in my VCAT proceeding properly and in time (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 114)? An offer can only be accepted by giving the party who made it a signed notice of acceptance, while the offer is open. You may accept even after making a counter-offer. No answer is given for an offer that names a period shorter than 14 days.
GIVEN `day offer made` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the offer was made (ISO date)
      `offer gives a period for acceptance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer specifies a period for acceptance
      `period for acceptance in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the offer gives for acceptance; 0 if none
      `signed notice of acceptance given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the party who made the offer a signed notice of acceptance
      `day of my notice` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you gave that notice (ISO date)
      `VCAT made its orders before my notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT had already made its orders on the matters in dispute before you gave the notice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did I accept the offer in time` MEANS
    `s 114 -- I accepted the offer in time` `day offer made` `offer gives a period for acceptance` `period for acceptance in days` `signed notice of acceptance given` `day of my notice` `VCAT made its orders before my notice`

DECLARE `Costs after a rejected offer` IS ONE OF
    `not covered: sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Sch 1 cl 22)`
    `no: section 112 does not apply to a proceeding for review of a decision (s 112(1)(a))`
    `no: VCAT cannot order costs in a proceeding relating to a small claim (Sch 1 cl 4I(1))`
    `no: the offer was not in writing, or does not comply with sections 113 and 114 (s 112(1)(a), (c))`
    `no: the offer was accepted while it was open (s 112(1)(b))`
    `no: in VCAT's opinion its orders are more favourable to the party who did not accept than the offer (s 112(1)(d))`
    `no: VCAT has ordered otherwise (s 112(2))`
    `yes: the party who did not accept the offer must pay all the costs the party who made it incurred after the offer was made (s 112(2))`

-- s 112(1) "This section applies if (a) a party ... (other than a proceeding for review of a decision) gives another party
-- an offer in writing ...; and (b) the other party does not accept the offer within the time the offer is open; and (c)
-- the offer complies with sections 113 and 114; and (d) in the opinion of the Tribunal, the orders made ... are not more
-- favourable to the other party than the offer." (2) "unless the Tribunal orders otherwise, a party who made an offer
-- ... is entitled to an order that the party who did not accept the offer pay all costs incurred by the offering party
-- after the offer was made." Small claims: cl 4I(1) "cannot order costs" (fork F5: cl 4I(1) prevails over s 112(2)).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `money` IS A BOOLEAN
      `says when paid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a period specified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period` IS A NUMBER
      `accepted in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `orders not more favourable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Costs after a rejected offer`
`s 112 -- costs after a rejected offer` `the kind` `in writing` `money` `says when paid` `a period specified` `the period` `accepted in time` `orders not more favourable` `ordered otherwise` MEANS
    IF `is under the Equal Opportunity Act` `the kind`
    THEN `not covered: sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Sch 1 cl 22)`
    ELSE IF `the kind` EQUALS `a review of a decision under another enabling enactment`
         THEN `no: section 112 does not apply to a proceeding for review of a decision (s 112(1)(a))`
         ELSE IF `is a small claim` `the kind`
              THEN `no: VCAT cannot order costs in a proceeding relating to a small claim (Sch 1 cl 4I(1))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `in writing`) OR (NOT (`ss 113, 114 -- the offer complies` `money` `says when paid` `a period specified` `the period`))
                   THEN `no: the offer was not in writing, or does not comply with sections 113 and 114 (s 112(1)(a), (c))`
                   ELSE IF `accepted in time`
                        THEN `no: the offer was accepted while it was open (s 112(1)(b))`
                        ELSE IF NOT `orders not more favourable`
                             THEN `no: in VCAT's opinion its orders are more favourable to the party who did not accept than the offer (s 112(1)(d))`
                             ELSE IF `ordered otherwise`
                                  THEN `no: VCAT has ordered otherwise (s 112(2))`
                                  ELSE `yes: the party who did not accept the offer must pay all the costs the party who made it incurred after the offer was made (s 112(2))`

@export I made a written offer to settle my VCAT proceeding and the other party did not accept it: am I entitled to an order that they pay my costs from the day of the offer (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 112, Sch 1 cls 22, 93, 4I)? Yes, unless VCAT orders otherwise, if the offer complied with the Act, was not accepted while open, and VCAT's orders are no more favourable to the other party than your offer. Not in a review of a decision (except a Transport Accident Act s 77 review), an Equal Opportunity Act proceeding or a consumer small claim.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `offer in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your offer was in writing
      `offer is to pay money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your offer provided for the payment of money
      `says when the money is paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it said when that money would be paid
      `offer gave a period for acceptance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your offer specified a period for acceptance
      `period for acceptance in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days your offer gave for acceptance; 0 if none
      `the other party accepted in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other party accepted your offer while it was open
      `orders no more favourable to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in VCAT's opinion, the orders it made are not more favourable to the other party than your offer (taking into account the costs it would have ordered on the day of the offer)
      `VCAT ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has ordered otherwise
GIVETH A `Costs after a rejected offer`
`Am I entitled to costs after my offer was rejected` MEANS
    `s 112 -- costs after a rejected offer` `kind of proceeding` `offer in writing` `offer is to pay money` `says when the money is paid` `offer gave a period for acceptance` `period for acceptance in days` `the other party accepted in time` `orders no more favourable to them` `VCAT ordered otherwise`

@export I did not accept the other party's written offer to settle my VCAT proceeding: must I pay their costs from the day of the offer (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 112, Sch 1 cls 22, 93, 4I)? Yes, unless VCAT orders otherwise, if the offer complied with the Act and VCAT's orders are no more favourable to you than the offer was. Not in a review of a decision (except a Transport Accident Act s 77 review), an Equal Opportunity Act proceeding or a consumer small claim.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `offer in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their offer was in writing
      `offer is to pay money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their offer provided for the payment of money
      `says when the money is paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it said when that money would be paid
      `offer gave a period for acceptance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their offer specified a period for acceptance
      `period for acceptance in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days their offer gave for acceptance; 0 if none
      `I accepted in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you accepted the offer, by signed notice, while it was open
      `orders no more favourable to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in VCAT's opinion, the orders it made are not more favourable to you than the offer was
      `VCAT ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has ordered otherwise
GIVETH A `Costs after a rejected offer`
`Must I pay costs after rejecting an offer` MEANS
    `s 112 -- costs after a rejected offer` `kind of proceeding` `offer in writing` `offer is to pay money` `says when the money is paid` `offer gave a period for acceptance` `period for acceptance in days` `I accepted in time` `orders no more favourable to me` `VCAT ordered otherwise`

DECLARE `An accepted offer not kept` IS ONE OF
    `not covered: sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Sch 1 cl 22)`
    `VCAT may, at my request, make an order giving effect to the terms of the offer (s 115(1)(a))`
    `VCAT may, at my request, give effect to the terms of the offer, or dismiss the proceeding (s 115(1)(a), (b)(i))`
    `VCAT may, at my request, give effect to the terms of the offer, dismiss the proceeding, or award me any or all of what I asked for in my counterclaim (s 115(1)(a), (b))`
    `VCAT may, at my request, give effect to the terms of the offer, or award me any or all of what I asked for in my application (s 115(1)(a), (c))`

-- s 115(1) "If an offer is accepted, but the party who made the offer does not comply with its terms, the Tribunal, at
-- the request of the party who accepted the offer, may (a) make an order giving effect to the terms of the offer; or (b)
-- if the party making the offer was the applicant (i) dismiss the proceeding; or (ii) if the party who accepted the offer
-- made a counterclaim before the offer was made, make an order awarding the party any or all of the things asked for in
-- the counterclaim; or (c) if the party who accepted the offer is the applicant, make an order awarding the applicant any
-- or all of the things asked for in the application."
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `the offering party was the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I counterclaimed before the offer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `An accepted offer not kept`
`s 115 -- an accepted offer not kept` `the kind` `the offering party was the applicant` `I am the applicant` `I counterclaimed before the offer` MEANS
    IF `is under the Equal Opportunity Act` `the kind`
    THEN `not covered: sections 112 to 115 do not apply to a proceeding under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Sch 1 cl 22)`
    ELSE IF `the offering party was the applicant`
         THEN IF `I counterclaimed before the offer`
              THEN `VCAT may, at my request, give effect to the terms of the offer, dismiss the proceeding, or award me any or all of what I asked for in my counterclaim (s 115(1)(a), (b))`
              ELSE `VCAT may, at my request, give effect to the terms of the offer, or dismiss the proceeding (s 115(1)(a), (b)(i))`
         ELSE IF `I am the applicant`
              THEN `VCAT may, at my request, give effect to the terms of the offer, or award me any or all of what I asked for in my application (s 115(1)(a), (c))`
              ELSE `VCAT may, at my request, make an order giving effect to the terms of the offer (s 115(1)(a))`

@export I accepted a settlement offer in my VCAT proceeding but the party who made it has not kept to its terms: what can VCAT do at my request (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 115)? It may order that the offer's terms be carried out; if the offer came from the applicant, dismiss the proceeding (or grant your earlier counterclaim); if you are the applicant, award you what you asked for in your application.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `the offer was made by the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party who made the offer is the applicant (the person who brought the proceeding)
      `I am the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the applicant
      `I counterclaimed before the offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made a counterclaim before the offer was made
GIVETH A `An accepted offer not kept`
`What can VCAT do if an accepted offer is not kept` MEANS
    `s 115 -- an accepted offer not kept` `kind of proceeding` `the offer was made by the applicant` `I am the applicant` `I counterclaimed before the offer`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Costs and fees at the end (ss 109, 115B, 115C, Sch 1 cls 4I, 12B, 92)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `My costs exposure` IS ONE OF
    `not covered: section 109 does not apply to a proceeding under the Transport Accident Act 1986 (Sch 1 cl 92); costs follow that Act`
    `no: VCAT cannot order costs in a proceeding relating to a small claim (Sch 1 cl 4I(1))`
    `yes: VCAT must award costs against me, as I was unsuccessful and refused, or did not in good faith take part in, the conciliation conference the dispute was referred to (Sch 1 cl 12B(2))`
    `possibly: VCAT may order me to pay all or a specified part of another party's costs, as it is satisfied that it is fair to do so (s 109(2), (3))`
    `no: each party bears their own costs, as VCAT is not satisfied that it is fair to order otherwise (s 109(1), (3))`

-- s 109(1) "Subject to this Division, each party is to bear their own costs"; (2), (3) "The Tribunal may make an order
-- under subsection (2) only if satisfied that it is fair to do so, having regard to" (a)-(e). Sch 1 cl 12B(2) "the
-- Tribunal must make an award of costs against an unsuccessful party to the dispute if the dispute had been referred for
-- a conciliation conference and the party (a) refused to participate ...; or (b) did not participate ... in good
-- faith", unless (3) VCAT "is satisfied that it would be unfair". Where cl 12B(3) excuses, s 109 decides (fork F6).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `a review of a s 120 determination` IS A BOOLEAN
      `unsuccessful` IS A BOOLEAN
      `refused or not in good faith at conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `VCAT satisfied it would be unfair` IS A BOOLEAN
      `VCAT satisfied it is fair` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `My costs exposure`
`ss 109, Sch 1 cls 4I, 12B, 92 -- my costs exposure` `the kind` `a review of a s 120 determination` `unsuccessful` `refused or not in good faith at conciliation` `VCAT satisfied it would be unfair` `VCAT satisfied it is fair` MEANS
    IF `is under the Transport Accident Act` `the kind`
    THEN `not covered: section 109 does not apply to a proceeding under the Transport Accident Act 1986 (Sch 1 cl 92); costs follow that Act`
    ELSE IF `is a small claim` `the kind` AND (NOT `a review of a s 120 determination`)
         THEN `no: VCAT cannot order costs in a proceeding relating to a small claim (Sch 1 cl 4I(1))`
         ELSE IF     `the kind` EQUALS `a domestic building work dispute under the Domestic Building Contracts Act 1995`
                 AND `unsuccessful`
                 AND `refused or not in good faith at conciliation`
                 AND (NOT `VCAT satisfied it would be unfair`)
              THEN `yes: VCAT must award costs against me, as I was unsuccessful and refused, or did not in good faith take part in, the conciliation conference the dispute was referred to (Sch 1 cl 12B(2))`
              ELSE IF `VCAT satisfied it is fair`
                   THEN `possibly: VCAT may order me to pay all or a specified part of another party's costs, as it is satisfied that it is fair to do so (s 109(2), (3))`
                   ELSE `no: each party bears their own costs, as VCAT is not satisfied that it is fair to order otherwise (s 109(1), (3))`

@export Will I have to pay the other party's legal costs in my VCAT proceeding (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 109, Sch 1 cls 4I, 12B, 92)? Each party bears their own costs unless VCAT is satisfied it is fair to order otherwise (having regard to conduct, delay, the strength of the claims and the complexity). No costs in a consumer small claim. In a domestic building work dispute an unsuccessful party who refused (or did not in good faith join) conciliation must pay costs unless that would be unfair. Transport Accident Act proceedings follow that Act. (Separate rules apply after a rejected settlement offer: s 112.)
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `a review of a section 120 determination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a small claim: whether this is a review (re-opening) under s 120 of an order made in the small claim
      `I was unsuccessful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a domestic building work dispute: whether you were unsuccessful in the dispute
      `I refused or did not take part in good faith in conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a domestic building work dispute: whether the dispute had been referred for a conciliation conference and you refused to take part, or did not take part in good faith
      `VCAT satisfied an award would be unfair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a domestic building work dispute: whether VCAT is satisfied that awarding costs against you would be unfair (for example because of the other party's conduct or delay)
      `VCAT satisfied an order is fair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT is satisfied that it is fair to order you to pay another party's costs, having regard to how you conducted the proceeding, any unreasonable delay, the relative strengths of the claims, the nature and complexity of the proceeding and anything else relevant
GIVETH A `My costs exposure`
`Will I have to pay the other party's costs` MEANS
    `ss 109, Sch 1 cls 4I, 12B, 92 -- my costs exposure` `kind of proceeding` `a review of a section 120 determination` `I was unsuccessful` `I refused or did not take part in good faith in conciliation` `VCAT satisfied an award would be unfair` `VCAT satisfied an order is fair`

DECLARE `My fees back` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am entitled to an order that the other party reimburse me the whole of the fees I paid in the proceeding (s 115C(2))`
    `no: VCAT has ordered otherwise, having regard to the proceeding and the parties' conduct (s 115C(3))`
    `no presumption: I did not substantially succeed against the other party (s 115C(2))`
    `no presumption in this kind of proceeding: VCAT may still order the other party to reimburse my fees, having regard to the proceeding, the parties' conduct and the result (s 115B)`

-- s 115C(1) the proceedings: "(a) a proceeding relating to a small claim ...; (b) a proceeding under the Domestic Building
-- Contracts Act 1995; (c) a proceeding under the Owners Corporations Act 2006, other than ... section 191 ...; (d) ...
-- section 114, 120 or 149A of the Planning and Environment Act 1987; (e) a proceeding of a kind prescribed". (2) "a party
-- who has substantially succeeded against another party ... is entitled to an order under section 115B that the other
-- party reimburse the successful party the whole of any fees paid"; (3) "does not apply if the Tribunal orders
-- otherwise". The Domestic Building Contracts Act kind here is the domestic building work dispute (fork F7).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of proceeding`
      `substantially succeeded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `My fees back`
`s 115C -- my fees back` `the kind` `substantially succeeded` `ordered otherwise` MEANS
    IF NOT (    `is a small claim` `the kind`
            OR  `the kind` EQUALS `a domestic building work dispute under the Domestic Building Contracts Act 1995`
            OR  `the kind` EQUALS `a proceeding under the Owners Corporations Act 2006 (other than a review under its section 191), under section 114, 120 or 149A of the Planning and Environment Act 1987, or of a kind prescribed for section 115C`)
    THEN `no presumption in this kind of proceeding: VCAT may still order the other party to reimburse my fees, having regard to the proceeding, the parties' conduct and the result (s 115B)`
    ELSE IF NOT `substantially succeeded`
         THEN `no presumption: I did not substantially succeed against the other party (s 115C(2))`
         ELSE IF `ordered otherwise`
              THEN `no: VCAT has ordered otherwise, having regard to the proceeding and the parties' conduct (s 115C(3))`
              ELSE `yes: I am entitled to an order that the other party reimburse me the whole of the fees I paid in the proceeding (s 115C(2))`

@export Am I entitled to have the other party pay back the VCAT fees I paid (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 115B, 115C)? In a consumer small claim, a domestic building dispute, an owners corporation dispute and some planning enforcement proceedings, a party who substantially succeeds is entitled to have the whole of their fees reimbursed, unless VCAT orders otherwise. In other proceedings VCAT may order reimbursement at its discretion.
GIVEN `kind of proceeding` IS A `Kind of proceeding` @desc What kind of VCAT proceeding it is
      `I substantially succeeded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you substantially succeeded against the other party
      `VCAT ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has ordered otherwise (having regard to the nature of the proceeding and the parties' conduct, such as unreasonable delay or not following VCAT's orders)
GIVETH A `My fees back`
`Do I get my fees back` MEANS
    `s 115C -- my fees back` `kind of proceeding` `I substantially succeeded` `VCAT ordered otherwise`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `VCAT's orders and reasons (ss 117, 120, 121, 122) -- once VCAT has made an order`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 117(1) "The Tribunal must give reasons for any order it makes in a proceeding, other than an interim order, within
-- (a) 60 days after making the order; or (b) such other period as is specified by the rules or the President." An
-- interim order: the text excludes it (NOTHING, no deadline).
@export By when must VCAT give reasons for its order in my proceeding (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 117(1))? Within 60 days after making the order, unless the rules or the President set another period. No date is given for an interim order, for which reasons are not required.
GIVEN `day of the order` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT made the order (ISO date)
      `interim order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order is an interim order
      `other period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The period for reasons that VCAT's rules or the President have set instead of 60 days, in days; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must VCAT give reasons` MEANS
    IF `interim order`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `other period in days` GREATER THAN 0
         THEN JUST (`the date` `other period in days` `days after` `day of the order`)
         ELSE JUST (`the date` 60 `days after` `day of the order`)

-- s 117(2) "If the Tribunal gives oral reasons, a party, within 14 days, may request the Tribunal to give written
-- reasons." The 14 days are counted from the day the oral reasons were given (fork F8: the text names no start).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 117(2) -- request written reasons within 14 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `request written reasons for the order`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the request for written reasons was not made within 14 days (s 117(2))"

@export VCAT gave oral reasons for its order: what is the last day on which I can ask for written reasons (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 117(2))? Within 14 days (counted here from the day the oral reasons were given). Asking also moves the start of the time to seek leave to appeal to the day the written reasons are given (s 148(4)).
GIVEN `day of the oral reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT gave its oral reasons (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day to ask for written reasons` MEANS
    `the date` 14 `days after` `day of the oral reasons`

@export Did I ask VCAT for written reasons in time after it gave oral reasons (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 117(2))? The request must be made within 14 days (counted here from the day of the oral reasons).
GIVEN `day of the oral reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT gave its oral reasons (ISO date)
      `day of my request` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you asked for written reasons (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did I ask for written reasons in time` MEANS
    IF `the day` `day of my request` `is before` `day of the oral reasons`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 117(2) -- request written reasons within 14 days` 14) `by` `I` `doing` `request written reasons for the order` `at` (`the days from` `day of the oral reasons` `to` `day of my request`))

-- s 117(3) "The Tribunal must comply with a request under subsection (2) within 45 days after receiving it." (4) "The
-- President may extend the 45-day period".
@export By when must VCAT give me the written reasons I asked for (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 117(3), (4))? Within 45 days after VCAT receives your request, unless the President extends that period (and gives you reasons for the extension).
GIVEN `day VCAT received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT received your request for written reasons (ISO date)
      `extension in days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days the President has extended the 45-day period by; 0 if not extended
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must VCAT give written reasons` MEANS
    `the date` (45 PLUS `extension in days`) `days after` `day VCAT received my request`

DECLARE `Re-opening an order made in my absence` IS ONE OF
    `no: I am not a person in respect of whom the order was made (s 120(1))`
    `no: I appeared or was represented at the hearing at which the order was made (s 120(1))`
    `I may apply (within the time the rules set), but VCAT will not hear it unless satisfied that I had a reasonable excuse for not attending or being represented (s 120(4)(a)(i))`
    `I may apply (within the time the rules set), but VCAT will not hear it unless satisfied that it is appropriate to, having regard to whether I have a reasonable case and any prejudice to another party (s 120(4)(a)(ii), (4A), (4B))`
    `I may apply, and VCAT may hear the application and, if it thinks fit, revoke or vary the order; it may require an undertaking as to costs or damages (s 120(4), (4C))`

-- s 120(1) "A person in respect of whom an order is made may apply ... if the person did not appear and was not represented
-- at the hearing"; (4) "The Tribunal may (a) hear and determine the application if it is satisfied that (i) the applicant
-- had a reasonable excuse ...; and (ii) it is appropriate ... having regard to the matters specified in subsection (4A);
-- and (b) if it thinks fit, order that the order be revoked or varied." (4B) prejudice that costs or a fee refund can
-- address. The time limits are in the rules (not deposited).
GIVEN `in respect of me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `appeared or represented` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
      `appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Re-opening an order made in my absence`
`s 120 -- re-opening an order made in my absence` `in respect of me` `appeared or represented` `reasonable excuse` `appropriate` MEANS
    IF NOT `in respect of me`
    THEN `no: I am not a person in respect of whom the order was made (s 120(1))`
    ELSE IF `appeared or represented`
         THEN `no: I appeared or was represented at the hearing at which the order was made (s 120(1))`
         ELSE IF NOT `reasonable excuse`
              THEN `I may apply (within the time the rules set), but VCAT will not hear it unless satisfied that I had a reasonable excuse for not attending or being represented (s 120(4)(a)(i))`
              ELSE IF NOT `appropriate`
                   THEN `I may apply (within the time the rules set), but VCAT will not hear it unless satisfied that it is appropriate to, having regard to whether I have a reasonable case and any prejudice to another party (s 120(4)(a)(ii), (4A), (4B))`
                   ELSE `I may apply, and VCAT may hear the application and, if it thinks fit, revoke or vary the order; it may require an undertaking as to costs or damages (s 120(4), (4C))`

@export VCAT made an order against me at a hearing I did not attend: can I have it re-opened (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 120)? If you neither appeared nor were represented, you may apply (within the time VCAT's rules set) for a review of the order. VCAT will hear it if satisfied you had a reasonable excuse and it is appropriate to, having regard to whether you have a reasonable case and any prejudice to the other party.
GIVEN `the order was made about me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person in respect of whom the order was made
      `I appeared or was represented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you appeared, or were represented, at the hearing at which the order was made
      `VCAT satisfied I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT is satisfied that you had a reasonable excuse for not attending or being represented at that hearing
      `VCAT satisfied it is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT is satisfied that it is appropriate to hear your application, having regard to whether you have a reasonable case to argue and any prejudice to another party (or that any prejudice can be met by an order for costs or a refund of fees)
GIVETH A `Re-opening an order made in my absence`
`Can I have an order made in my absence re-opened` MEANS
    `s 120 -- re-opening an order made in my absence` `the order was made about me` `I appeared or was represented` `VCAT satisfied I had a reasonable excuse` `VCAT satisfied it is appropriate`

DECLARE `Where I enforce the order` IS ONE OF
    `in the Magistrates' Court, as the amount owing is within its jurisdictional limit; the order is taken to be an order of that court (s 121)`
    `in the County Court or the Supreme Court; the order is taken to be an order of the court I choose (s 121)`
    `in the Supreme Court; the order is taken to be an order of the Supreme Court (s 122)`

-- s 121(1) "A person in whose favour a monetary order is made may enforce the order in (a) if the amount owing under the
-- order is within the jurisdictional limit of the Magistrates' Court, the Magistrates' Court; or (b) otherwise, either
-- the County Court or the Supreme Court." s 122(1) a non-monetary order "in the Supreme Court". The Magistrates' Court's
-- limit is set by another Act (borrowed: asked); "within" the limit includes the limit itself (fork F9).
GIVEN `monetary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amount owing` IS A NUMBER
      `the limit` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Where I enforce the order`
`ss 121, 122 -- where I enforce the order` `monetary` `the amount owing` `the limit` MEANS
    IF NOT `monetary`
    THEN `in the Supreme Court; the order is taken to be an order of the Supreme Court (s 122)`
    ELSE IF `the amount owing` AT MOST `the limit`
         THEN `in the Magistrates' Court, as the amount owing is within its jurisdictional limit; the order is taken to be an order of that court (s 121)`
         ELSE `in the County Court or the Supreme Court; the order is taken to be an order of the court I choose (s 121)`

@export VCAT made an order in my favour that has not been complied with: in which court can I enforce it (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 ss 121, 122)? A money order within the Magistrates' Court's jurisdictional limit is enforced there, a larger one in the County Court or Supreme Court; any other order in the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `money order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order is for the payment of money
      `amount owing` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount still owing under the order, in dollars (0 if it is not a money order)
      `Magistrates' Court limit` IS A NUMBER @desc The Magistrates' Court's jurisdictional limit for this kind of claim, in dollars, as set by the Magistrates' Court Act 1989
GIVETH A `Where I enforce the order`
`Where can I enforce my VCAT order` MEANS
    `ss 121, 122 -- where I enforce the order` `money order` `amount owing` `Magistrates' Court limit`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Appeals (s 148) -- if I want to appeal VCAT's order`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `My appeal` IS ONE OF
    `to the Court of Appeal, with the leave of the Court of Appeal, on a question of law (s 148(1)(a))`
    `to the Trial Division of the Supreme Court, with the leave of the Trial Division, on a question of law (s 148(1)(b))`
    `not covered: section 148 gives an appeal on a question of law`

-- s 148(1) "A party to a proceeding may appeal on a question of law from an order of the Tribunal ... (a) if the Tribunal
-- was constituted ... by the President or a Vice President, whether with or without others, to the Court of Appeal with
-- leave of the Court of Appeal; or (b) in any other case, to the Trial Division of the Supreme Court with leave of the
-- Trial Division." A grant limited in its own wording (on a question of law): NOTHING outside it.
GIVEN `on a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `President or Vice President` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `My appeal`
`s 148(1) -- my appeal` `on a question of law` `President or Vice President` MEANS
    IF NOT `on a question of law`
    THEN `not covered: section 148 gives an appeal on a question of law`
    ELSE IF `President or Vice President`
         THEN `to the Court of Appeal, with the leave of the Court of Appeal, on a question of law (s 148(1)(a))`
         ELSE `to the Trial Division of the Supreme Court, with the leave of the Trial Division, on a question of law (s 148(1)(b))`

@export Can I appeal VCAT's order, and to which court (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 148(1))? A party may appeal only on a question of law, and only with leave: to the Court of Appeal if VCAT was constituted by its President or a Vice President, otherwise to the Trial Division of the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `on a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your appeal is on a question of law (a question about what the law is or how it applies, including a mixed question of law and fact), not just a disagreement with VCAT's view of the facts
      `VCAT was the President or a Vice President` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT was constituted for making the order by its President or a Vice President (a Supreme Court or County Court judge), with or without others
GIVETH A `My appeal`
`Where does my appeal go` MEANS
    `s 148(1) -- my appeal` `on a question of law` `VCAT was the President or a Vice President`

-- s 148(2) "An application for leave to appeal to the Trial Division ... must be made (a) within 28 days after the day of
-- the order of the Tribunal, unless the rules of the Supreme Court otherwise provide"; (4) "If the Tribunal gives oral
-- reasons ... and a party then requests it to give written reasons under section 117, the day on which the written
-- reasons are given to the party is deemed to be the day of the order for the purposes of subsection (2)." The Supreme
-- Court's rules and the Court of Appeal's time limits (Supreme Court Act 1986) are not deposited.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 148(2) -- apply for leave within 28 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `apply for leave to appeal`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the application for leave to appeal was not made within 28 days after the day of the order (s 148(2)(a))"

GIVEN `the day of the order` IS A DATE
      `written reasons requested` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day written reasons given` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 148(4) -- the day of the order for the time limit` `the day of the order` `written reasons requested` `the day written reasons given` MEANS
    IF `written reasons requested`
    THEN `the day written reasons given`
    ELSE `the day of the order`

@export What is the last day on which I can apply to the Supreme Court for leave to appeal VCAT's order (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 148(2), (4))? Within 28 days after the day of the order (unless the Supreme Court's rules provide otherwise); if VCAT gave oral reasons and you asked for written reasons, the 28 days run from the day the written reasons were given to you. The Trial Division may extend the time. No date is given for an appeal to the Court of Appeal, whose time limits are in the Supreme Court Act 1986.
GIVEN `VCAT was the President or a Vice President` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT was constituted for making the order by its President or a Vice President (the appeal then goes to the Court of Appeal)
      `day of the order` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT made the order (ISO date)
      `I asked for written reasons after oral reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT gave oral reasons and you then asked for written reasons under s 117
      `day written reasons given to me` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the written reasons were given to you (ISO date); any date if not asked for
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`What is the last day to apply for leave to appeal` MEANS
    IF `VCAT was the President or a Vice President`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the date` 28 `days after` (`s 148(4) -- the day of the order for the time limit` `day of the order` `I asked for written reasons after oral reasons` `day written reasons given to me`))

@export Did I apply to the Supreme Court for leave to appeal VCAT's order in time (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 148(2), (4))? The application must be made within 28 days after the day of the order, or after the day written reasons were given if you asked for them after oral reasons. If you were late, the Trial Division may still extend the time (s 148(5)). No answer is given for an appeal to the Court of Appeal.
GIVEN `VCAT was the President or a Vice President` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT was constituted for making the order by its President or a Vice President
      `day of the order` IS A DATE @desc The day on which VCAT made the order (ISO date)
      `I asked for written reasons after oral reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT gave oral reasons and you then asked for written reasons under s 117
      `day written reasons given to me` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the written reasons were given to you (ISO date); any date if not asked for
      `day I applied for leave` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you applied for leave to appeal (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did I apply for leave to appeal in time` MEANS
    IF `VCAT was the President or a Vice President`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the day` `day I applied for leave` `is before` `the start day`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 148(2) -- apply for leave within 28 days` 28) `by` `I` `doing` `apply for leave to appeal` `at` (`the days from` `the start day` `to` `day I applied for leave`))
    WHERE
        `the start day` MEANS `s 148(4) -- the day of the order for the time limit` `day of the order` `I asked for written reasons after oral reasons` `day written reasons given to me`

-- s 148(3) "If leave to appeal to the Trial Division ... is granted, the appeal must be instituted (a) no later than 14
-- days after the day on which leave is granted".
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 148(3) -- institute the appeal within 14 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `institute the appeal`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the appeal was not instituted within 14 days after the day on which leave was granted (s 148(3)(a))"

@export The Supreme Court's Trial Division has granted me leave to appeal VCAT's order: what is the last day to institute the appeal (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 148(3))? No later than 14 days after the day leave is granted. You must also notify VCAT's principal registrar that you have instituted it (s 148(6)).
GIVEN `day leave granted` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the Trial Division granted leave to appeal (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day to institute my appeal` MEANS
    `the date` 14 `days after` `day leave granted`

@export Did I institute my appeal from VCAT in time after the Trial Division granted leave (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 148(3))? It must be instituted no later than 14 days after the day leave was granted (the Trial Division may extend the time: s 148(5)).
GIVEN `day leave granted` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the Trial Division granted leave to appeal (ISO date)
      `day appeal instituted` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you instituted the appeal (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did I institute my appeal in time` MEANS
    IF `the day` `day appeal instituted` `is before` `day leave granted`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 148(3) -- institute the appeal within 14 days` 14) `by` `I` `doing` `institute the appeal` `at` (`the days from` `day leave granted` `to` `day appeal instituted`))

-- s 148(9) "A party to a proceeding under a credit enactment that involves a claim not exceeding $3000 cannot apply for
-- leave to appeal under this section unless that party agrees to indemnify the reasonable legal costs of the other
-- parties"; (10) credit enactment: the Credit Act 1984, s 45 of the Motor Car Traders Act 1986.
`the s 148(9) claim limit` MEANS 3000

@export In a VCAT proceeding under the Credit Act 1984 or section 45 of the Motor Car Traders Act 1986 over a claim of $3000 or less, can I apply for leave to appeal (Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 1998 s 148(9), (10))? Only if you agree to indemnify the other parties' reasonable legal costs. No answer is given for other proceedings, to which this rule does not apply.
GIVEN `under a credit enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding was under the Credit Act 1984 or section 45 of the Motor Car Traders Act 1986
      `amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the claim in the proceeding, in dollars
      `I agree to indemnify their costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agree to indemnify the reasonable legal costs of the other parties
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I seek leave to appeal in a small credit claim` MEANS
    IF NOT `under a credit enactment`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `amount claimed` AT MOST `the s 148(9) claim limit`
         THEN JUST `I agree to indemnify their costs`
         ELSE JUST TRUE
